At 6:41 PM 11/29/94, Phil Goetz wrote:
>Your response was interesting.  How do you propose to privatize everything?

There are a thousand methods.  One considered by the Poles when they turned
out the Communists was:

 1) Establish each factory, farm, etc. as a corporation.
 2) Establish a number of mutual funds (say, ten), each of which holds one-
    tenth of the shares in each corporation.
 3) Since the means of production are supposedly "the common heritage of
    the people of Poland," give each citizen 1/N of the shares of each of
    the ten mutual funds, where N is the population of Poland.
 4) Thereafter, allow free trading in any of the shares -- mutual funds,
    corporations, and individuals can all buy or sell shares in any or all
    of the corporations or funds.

A variant on this has been proposed as the distribution mechanism for the
U.N. Moon Treaty.

>We could sell all the government's assets; then what would happen to that
>money when we eliminate the government?

One proposal is to make one-time settlement payments, in lieu of continued
periodic payments, to those presently holding open-ended entitlements
(Social Security, military pensions, etc.).  These entitlements represent
an $11-trillion commitment on the part of the federal government which is
_not_ counted as part of the conventional "national debt" computations.
Many of them are in fact owed to the recipients because over the years they
paid in FICA taxes, etc., to the system.  Increased life expectancy and
inflation have made it impossible for anyone to get back out of SS anywhere
near what they paid in; at least this way, young taxpayers (like me) can
get _something_ rather than nothing.

>(BTW, who prints new money when there's no government?
>Banks?  That didn't work well in the 19th century.)

How so?  Inflation has devalued the currency a _hell_ of a lot more since
the Federal Reserve was established.  Fractional reserve banking is a
painfully inflationary mechanism.  (Inflation is a hidden tax, because, by
creating new dollar bills out of thin air to spend, the State reduces the
value of the dollars in your pocket.)

The ideal currency system would be multiple competing currencies backed by
a variety of issuers.  It really doesn't matter what one uses for money, so
long as it's (a) not easily forged (doesn't "grow on trees"), and (b)
backed by some commodity that's valuable in itself.  Any token which some
reputable agency (big bank, big corporation, Roman Catholic Church,
Microsoft, Medellin cartel, whatever) has committed to redeem for {gold,
uranium, wheat, cocaine, whatever} will work as a currency.  The various
currencies (and the agencies backing them) will compete in the reputation
marketplace, at floating exchange rates... as some issuers acquire negative
reputations, participants in the market will get out of those currencies
and into the more reputable ones.  (Gresham's Law only applies when a State
is decreeing two competing currencies to have a fixed exchange rate.)

We already have a system of competing currencies at the global level; at
present, the only agencies backing currencies are states, but that's just
because every state prohibits anyone within its borders from issuing new
currencies -- in a free market, there'll be currencies backed by all kinds
of entities and commodities, although I suspect, in a post-nanotech world,
the individual chemical elements will become the major backing
commodities... you can make more cocaine, but you can't (easily) make more
carbon atoms!

Henry Hazlitt's "Economics In One Lesson" is a great introduction to
economic issues.  You can get it from Laissez-Faire books in San Francisco
(800-326-0996) or Freedom's Forum bookstore (whose number I don't have
handy).  Specifically regarding the issue of currency, I'm told that "Free
Banking" by Charles Duncombe is quite good, though I haven't read it
myself.
