From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 30 Nov 94 14:45:51 EST
Subject: [#94-11-530] NANOTECH: Closure

Nick Szabo has discussed the difficulties of replication and the
desirability of maximizing closure. The following quote is relevant to
thinking about these issues and about how long it will take to develop
nanotech: 

Quote from a report on Foresight Institute Senior Associate Gathering

Ralph (Merkle) briefly reviewed the hydrogen abstraction tool designed and
modeled by Charles Musgrave (last year's Feynmann prize winner), and
mentioned a useful force field model recently developed at the Naval
Research Lab (NRL).  Using these as starting points, we now think that
we can build almost any rigid hydrocarbon structure with just four
tools: hydrogen abstraction, hydrogen deposition, carbene insertion,
and dimmer deposition (two carbon atoms inserted at a time).  These
tools are being designed and modeled now.  One possible negative
discovery from this work is that it appears we may need an assembler
with a greater range of motion than that provided by a simple Stewart
platform.  In any case, this is getting to be an extremely exciting
area of investigation.

End quote

A key part of this quote is `Using these as starting points, we now think we
can build almost any rigid hydrocarbon structure with just four tools'.
Perhaps one can complain that this comment is premature or overly optimistic;
however, major portions of a nanotech system can probably be built to fit
into this capacious class of potentially buildable systems.  This is an
excellent advance.  Huge numbers of problems remain to be solved.  The quote
itself mentions the need to come up with an assembler manipulator more
capable than a simple Stewart platform. Nick Szabo has identified many
concerns and correctly states that only a sketch has been provided by
Drexler and Merkle.  It is even unclear if Drexler has identified the best
overall direction.  But work has just begun and significant strides are
already being made in my opinion. Of course, some of these strides will lead
into dead ends.  Millions of dollars are being spent on work relevant to
nanotech (Actually, tens of millions, or hundreds of millions depending on
the breadth of your definitions).  There are payoffs along the way and
funding will increase as the applicability (and perceived near-term
applicability) of systems increases. In the future, billions, tens of
billions and more will be spent.

Developing nanotech is a large task; but the assertion that it will take more
than one hundred years seems unjustified to me.  One hundred years is a very
long time in the modern scientific and technological era. Scientific workers
and publications have been growing exponentially (though this rate of growth
may not continue).  Nanotech is a multidisciplinary area and I do not think
any single person has fully mastered all the relevant scientific
subdisciplines - I certainly have not. My doctorate is in computer science.
My opinion is effected by the opinions of people like Drexler and Merkle and
by the knowledge that I have acquired. It would be interesting to hear the
opinions and reasoning of people like: Goddard of Caltech, Musgrave
(formerly of Caltech), Dr. Don Eigler of IBM (who spelled the word IBM using
individual atoms with an STM), Dr.  Heinrich Rohrer also of IBM (coinventor
of the STM), and Dr.  Clayton Teague, editor of the journal Nanotechnology
and others.

There already exists a remarkable replicating system which displays enough
closure to create a multiplicity of creatures all over Earth.  The system is
based on stringing together amino acids to form proteins.  We are learning
more about this system and how to control it all the time.  In many ways it
is a crude, kludgy and limited system and yet it can build human brains!
How much closure does the system display? Well, humans have to eat vitamins
and minerals along with amino acids and water etc. - after all, you do need
iron in your hemoglobin.  But the system works.  I am not saying that a
nanotech manufacturing system is going to be patterned closely on cellular
processes (though cellular processes may be exploited during the bootstrap
process and cell repair machines may tap into it).  It is just interesting
that watery bags with Rube Goldberg metabolisms constructed blindly by
variation and selection are the nanotech systems which rule the planet.

The phrase `develop nanotech' is ambiguous as Nick Szabo notes; however, the
`specific technologies' he lists below do not always reduce the ambiguity by
very much.

Szabo says:

Here are _specific_ technologies that probably won't appear within the next
one hundred years:

* Self-reproducing diamondoid machines with sufficient closure to 
bring most of the postulated the benefits of exponential growth.
* Utility fog.
* Cell repair machines.
* Cryonics reanimation.
* AI or uploading (although computers and robots will be much faster 
	and more sophisticated).

End quote

`Machines' which perform some of the tasks of cell repair already exist
naturally today, e.g., for DNA repair, and we will learn more about how to
manipulate and modify them.  AI programs are already in use performing
valuable tasks.  These two areas will likely experience improvements along a
continuum so I do not think they are ideal for providing a demarcation for
nanotech.  Self-reproducing diamondoid machines are a provisional target and
it is unclear if they will remain the target.  Also, growth need not be
exponential to get dramatic benefits.

Prediction: It will be possible to produce several tons of material using
nanotech assemblers with positional control analogous to those described in
Nanosystems within fifty years.

I think this prediction is conservative. Gregory Sullivan

