Subject: [#94-11-534] Social Security (was: BASICS: Re: environment)

Dave Krieger writes:
>Many of them are in fact owed to the recipients because over the years they
>paid in FICA taxes, etc., to the system.  Increased life expectancy and
>inflation have made it impossible for anyone to get back out of SS anywhere
>near what they paid in; at least this way, young taxpayers (like me) can
>get _something_ rather than nothing.

Actually, one of the problems with the SS system is that those currently
receiving SS payments are getting back *substantially* more than the actuarial
value of what they put in.  People who retired during the 1970's, in fact, not
only got back what they paid into the SS pool, but also, on average, all taxes
they ever paid in their entire life and then some.

It is basically a case of democracy gone way over the top, where one powerful
group gets to use the power of the State to plunder everybody else.  The AARP
is the the biggest, scariest and most powerful lobby in Washington -- one out
of every four registered voters is a member and they vote consistently against
any politician who even breathes mention of doing anything to SS.  A very
interesting and scary book about all this is _On Borrowed Time_ by Peter
Petersen.

The reality, of course, is that SS is a pyramid scheme rather than the
"insurance" program that its propogandists claim.  Although the picture painted
by Petersen's statistics suggests the younger taxpayers among us are just going
to get screwed when the pyramid comes crashing down, I suspect that something
else will happen in the meantime (nanotech, perhaps) to pull the politicos'
cojones out of the fire just in the nick of time.

